*{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0;}

body{background:#3a2a18;font-family:'EB Garamond',Georgia,serif;color:var(--ink);padding:40px 20px;}

.page{max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;background:var(--parchment);border:2px solid var(--border);border-radius:4px;box-shadow:0 8px 40px rgba(44,26,14,.18);padding:60px 70px;}

.verse { color: var(--accent-blue); font-weight: 500; }

.key-term { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent-deep); }

.inline-source { font-size: 0.88em; color: #666; }

.end-marker { text-align: center; font-style: italic; color: var(--accent-blue); margin: 30px 0; font-size: 17px; }

.halacha-note { text-align: center; font-style: italic; color: #888; margin: 20px 0; font-size: 16px; }

.nachnach { text-align: center; margin-top: 50px; font-size: 26px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 2px; color: var(--accent-gold); }

Likutay Halachos &mdash; Choshen Mishpat II

Hilchos Apitropos [Laws of Guardianship]

Halacha 3 &mdash; Part C (&sect;&sect;11&ndash;16)

&sect;11

And therefore after the seventh day of Pesach specifically, chametz is permitted. For we have already explained that the essential prohibition of chametz is because the air of the world has not yet been clarified from the voice of melody of the Sitra Achra, as above. And therefore, after the seventh day of Pesach, when the Sea split and they merited the voice of Shiras HaYam &mdash; through which the air of the world was clarified and purified, through the voice of holy song and melody being clarified from the voice of melody of the Sitra Achra &mdash; therefore, then certainly chametz is permitted, since the air of the world has already been clarified and purified through Shiras HaYam, as above.

